{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Let Him go, etc., for it is a people, etc.</b> It is difficult, for in the words of HaShem (Exodus 33:3) He said to him: ״לֹא אֶעֱלֶה בְּקִרְבְּךָ כִּי עַם קְשֵׁה עֹרֶף״ — if so, for the reason עַם קְשֵׁה עֹרֶף He would withhold the going up of HaShem in their midst, so how does Moses give the reason itself in order that He should go up? Further it is difficult: why was it necessary to ask something regarding which HaShem had already been appeased when he asked Him to go with them, and He answered him (Exodus 33:17): ״גַּם אֶת הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה וְגוֹ׳ אֶעֱשֶׂה״?" "Now, the matter will be explained with a further fine point: why did Moses say here the name Adnut (אֲדֹנָי), and the difficulty grows great according to what I explained above (Exodus 33:3), that Moses desired only the aspect of mercy." "Or it may be that when HaShem revealed to Moses His attributes, blessed be He, and he saw their immensity, he understood that if HaShem were to conduct Himself with Israel by these attributes without any justice whatsoever — as he had asked earlier when he passed the goodness before Him — they would not be able to stand on the straight path before HaShem, for it is a stiff-necked people. Therefore he retracted and asked a request that is for the sake of the King's honor, so that His goodness would not cause them to degrade His commandments, blessed be He, for when the aspect of justice is entirely removed from the wicked, they become contemptuous. Therefore he said <b>יֵלֶךְ נָא אֲדֹנָי</b> — which is the aspect of justice — and he asked for an attribute that he needed, as he said: <b>וְסָלַחְתָּ לַעֲוֹנֵנוּ</b>; and this attribute is not in the aspect of Adnut, and so it says (Exodus 23:21): ״כִּי לֹא יִשָּׂא לְפִשְׁעֲכֶם״ — except through the attribute of mercy. Thus you find that Moses desires both mercy and justice, for the two together are better than one alone." "<b>And our heritage.</b> Meaning, that He not exchange them for 